    <string name="help_section_state">CURRENT STATE</string>
    <string name="help_section_common">COMMON PROBLEMS</string>
    <string name="help_section_how">HOW IT WORKS</string>
    <string name="help_troubleshooting">If the Mac is not found on the network:\n• Is AndroMac running in the menu bar on your Mac?\n• Are both devices on the same Wi-Fi network? The phone may have fallen back to mobile data.\n• A guest network or client isolation may be on; devices cannot see each other on those.\n• macOS 15+ asks for \"Local Network\" permission on first launch. If it was denied: System Settings → Privacy and Security → Local Network.\n\nIf it connects but keeps dropping:\n• Exempt the app from battery optimization (Permissions card on the main screen).\n• \"AP isolation\" must be off on your router.\n\nIf notifications never arrive:\n• Has notification access been granted?\n• That app may be set to Off in the app filter.\n• Silent notifications are not sent by default.\n\nIf the notification access switch is greyed out:\nAndroid 13+ locks that switch for apps installed from a file (\"unavailable for your security\"). Try to turn it on once, then go to Settings → Apps → AndroMac → ⋮ (top right) → Allow restricted settings. The menu item only appears after you have tried. Then turn the switch on again.</string>
    <string name="help_how_it_works">Your Mac announces itself on the local network; the phone finds it and connects directly. There is no server in between and no data leaves for the internet.\n\nPairing shows a six-digit code on both screens. If the codes match, nobody is in the middle. After you approve, the Mac\'s key is pinned on the phone; if that key ever changes the connection is refused and you are warned.\n\nTraffic is end-to-end encrypted (P-256 ECDH + AES-256-GCM). Fresh keys are derived for every session.</string>

